===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# The [[nearness]] of objects with little or no [[delimiter]].
#: {{syn|en|apposition}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1809|author=w:Samuel Taylor Coleridge|journal=[https://archive.org/details/friendseriesofes01coleuoft/page/96/mode/2up The Friend]
|passage=It is the object of the mechanical atomistic philosophy to confound synthesis with synartesis, or rather with mere '''juxtaposition''' of corpuscles separated by invisible interspaces.}}
## {{lb|en|grammar}} An [[absence]] of [[link]]ing elements in a group of words that are [[list]]ed together.
##: Example: ''mother father'' instead of ''mother and father''
## {{lb|en|mathematics}} An absence of [[operator]]s in an expression.
##: {{ux|en|Using '''juxtaposition''' for multiplication saves space when writing longer expressions. <math>a \times b </math> collapses to <math>ab</math>.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2007|author=Lawrence Moss; Hans-Jörg Tiede|chapter=Applications of Modal Logic in Linguistics|editors=P. Blackburn; et al.|title=Handbook of Modal Logic|publisher=Elsevier|page=1054
|passage=A fundamental operation on strings is ''string concatenation'' which we will denote by '''juxtaposition'''.}}
# The extra [[emphasis]] given to a [[comparison]] when the contrasted objects are close together.
#: {{ux|en|There was a poignant '''juxtaposition''' between the boys laughing in the street and the girl crying on the balcony above.}}
## {{lb|en|arts}} Two or more contrasting [[sound]]s, [[colour]]s, [[style]]s etc. placed together for stylistic effect.
##: {{ux|en|The '''juxtaposition''' of the bright yellows on the dark background made the painting appear three dimensional.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2001|author=Dean R. Koontz|title=One Door Away from Heaven|publisher=Random House|isbn=9780553582758|pageurl=http://books.google.com/books?id=_klU1t2uqzEC&pg=PA497&dq=opisthenar|page=497
|passage=Her mother favored a multiyear project: obscenities carved in intricate and clever '''juxtapositions''', descending every finger, curling in lettered whorls across the palm, fanning in offensive rays across the opisthenar, which is the name for the back of the hand, a word that Leilani knew because she had studied the structure of the human hand in detail, the better to understand her difference.}}
## {{lb|en|rhetoric}} The close placement of two [[idea]]s to imply a link that may not exist.
##: ''Example: In 1965 the government was elected; in 1965 the economy took a dive.''

